WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.882
Temperature change alone still
does not prove a new substance.

00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:03.340
Exactly.

00:00:03.731 --> 00:00:06.684
Thermal decomposition often
needs sustained heating

00:00:06.684 --> 00:00:08.117
because energy is absorbed.

00:00:08.633 --> 00:00:13.115
Combustion and many neutralizations
release energy, although a reaction may

00:00:13.116 --> 00:00:15.189
need an initial input to get started.

00:00:15.935 --> 00:00:19.681
With enough oxygen, methane
forms carbon dioxide and water.

00:00:20.192 --> 00:00:22.628
What happens when the
oxygen supply is limited?

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:26.447
Incomplete combustion can
produce carbon monoxide and

00:00:26.448 --> 00:00:28.415
soot, along with other products.

00:00:28.901 --> 00:00:32.349
Carbon monoxide is colorless,
odorless, and toxic.

00:00:32.624 --> 00:00:35.217
It interferes with oxygen
transport in the blood.

00:00:35.967 --> 00:00:40.755
So no smell does not mean no
danger, and a flame's color is not

00:00:40.763 --> 00:00:42.829
a reliable carbon monoxide detector.

00:00:43.611 --> 00:00:44.182
Correct.

00:00:44.639 --> 00:00:48.617
Fuel, temperature, and other conditions
also affect flame appearance.

00:00:48.989 --> 00:00:52.699
Proper ventilation, correctly
maintained appliances, and working

00:00:52.701 --> 00:00:54.588
carbon monoxide alarms matter.

00:00:54.945 --> 00:00:58.789
We never deliberately restrict a
flame's oxygen to make a dangerous gas.

00:00:59.599 --> 00:01:01.158
Carbon monoxide is CO.

00:01:01.478 --> 00:01:03.376
Carbon dioxide is CO2.

00:01:03.882 --> 00:01:07.600
One small subscript changes
the substance and the hazards.

00:01:08.009 --> 00:01:11.940
Why can a powder react faster than a
lump of the same material and mass?

00:01:12.842 --> 00:01:16.933
More surface is exposed to the other
reactant, so more particles can

00:01:16.933 --> 00:01:21.207
collide at the surface, not because
powder contains more atoms overall.

00:01:21.984 --> 00:01:22.772
Exactly.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:26.658
Rate measures how quickly reactants
are used or products form.

00:01:27.018 --> 00:01:30.805
We might measure a gas volume per
unit time or track mass change

00:01:30.815 --> 00:01:32.812
in suitable supervised apparatus.

00:01:33.598 --> 00:01:36.670
Do particles react every time
they bump into each other?

00:01:37.466 --> 00:01:37.836
No.

00:01:38.371 --> 00:01:41.659
Collisions need enough energy
and a suitable arrangement.

00:01:42.155 --> 00:01:46.231
Raising temperature increases particle
energy, so more collisions can

00:01:46.252 --> 00:01:48.330
overcome the reaction's energy barrier.

00:01:48.632 --> 00:01:52.209
Higher concentration often
increases collision frequency when

00:01:52.229 --> 00:01:53.794
other conditions are comparable.

00:01:54.563 --> 00:01:58.960
For a fair comparison, change one
factor and control the others, such as

00:01:58.965 --> 00:02:01.372
mass, temperature, and solution volume.

00:02:01.837 --> 00:02:03.063
Repeat measurements too.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:04.172
Good.

00:02:04.684 --> 00:02:08.413
A catalyst offers an alternative
pathway with a lower energy barrier.

00:02:08.819 --> 00:02:11.232
It is not consumed
overall by the reaction.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:15.225
It does not supply unlimited
energy or change an exothermic

00:02:15.225 --> 00:02:17.050
reaction into an endothermic one.

00:02:17.799 --> 00:02:22.824
Enzymes are biological catalysts, and
catalytic converters help harmful exhaust

00:02:22.832 --> 00:02:25.332
gases react into less harmful products.

00:02:26.086 --> 00:02:26.684
Yes.

00:02:27.017 --> 00:02:30.073
Catalysts can take part in
steps and be regenerated.

00:02:30.395 --> 00:02:34.085
Real catalysts can also become
poisoned or lose effectiveness.

00:02:34.389 --> 00:02:38.907
Simply finding an object unchanged at the
end does not prove it catalyzed anything.

00:02:39.389 --> 00:02:41.589
We need evidence that
it increased the rate.

00:02:42.315 --> 00:02:45.750
Final rescue: Do bubbles
prove a chemical reaction?

00:02:46.021 --> 00:02:48.247
Can atoms disappear when gas escapes?

00:02:48.603 --> 00:02:51.252
And may we change a subscript
to balance an equation?

00:02:52.002 --> 00:02:53.065
No to all three.

00:02:53.462 --> 00:02:54.738
Bubbles can be physical.

00:02:55.107 --> 00:02:56.958
Escaping gas still has mass.

00:02:57.332 --> 00:02:59.516
Coefficients balance correct formulae.

00:02:59.843 --> 00:03:02.218
Changing a subscript
changes the substance.

00:03:03.021 --> 00:03:07.455
And if a catalyst makes a reaction
finish sooner, must it produce more

00:03:07.455 --> 00:03:09.916
product from the same limiting reactant?

00:03:10.594 --> 00:03:10.884
No.

00:03:11.434 --> 00:03:13.149
Faster is not the same as more.

00:03:13.404 --> 00:03:17.101
It changes the route and rate, not
the number of atoms we started with.

00:03:17.935 --> 00:03:18.633
Excellent.

00:03:19.151 --> 00:03:23.026
New substances, conserved atoms,
balanced equations, energy

00:03:23.027 --> 00:03:25.203
transfers, and successful collisions.

00:03:25.591 --> 00:03:29.704
That is a reaction story worth
explaining, not just memorizing
